    "q-43510": {
      "dom_id": "step-q43510",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices. \n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=-8{R}_{1},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=-8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are elementary matrices.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
